\date{2025-11-03T18:10:48Z}
\taxon{Remark}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{It's worth discussing the motivation for each of these components.}

\ul{
  \li{The capability set #{C} is used to record capabilities needed to execute certain commands, use certain language constructs, and so on.}
  \li{\p{The device set #{D} is used to record specific devices required by a \tt{require} directive.  For example, the \tt{boat} device confers the capability to traverse water, and does not correspond to a specific command or language construct, so the programmer needs a way to require it specifically.  Also, since multiple devices may provide a given capability, the programmer may have reasons for requiring a specific device rather than letting the system choose.}
  \p{One might still wonder why we need both #{C} and #{D}; wouldn't it be simpler to just have a set of required devices #{D}? However, since the relationship between devices and capabilities can be many-to-many, we cannot reliably pick a concrete device for a required capability in a nicely compositional way.  We prefer to wait until the last possible minute to pick a concrete set of devices to provides a given set of capabilities.}
  }
  \li{The inventory multiset #{I} has several purposes.  One is simply to record inventory required by a \tt{stock} directive.  For example, \tt{stock 10 "rock"} requires a robot to have 10 rocks in its inventory.  More importantly, however, the \tt{build} command turns device requirements into inventory requirements.  That is, if a robot must have device #{d} equipped in order to execute program \tt{P}, then in order to execute \tt{build{P}}, a robot must have device #{d} in its inventory (so that it can equip the device on the newly constructed robot).}
}
